    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "reviewer_critique",
      "value": "The submission lacks a formal validation protocol: there is no specification of test thresholds, no statistical significance assessment (e.g., confidence intervals on held-out error), and no convergence analysis with respect to width/depth hyperparameters (w5, h2–64). The held-out errors increase with network depth (e.g., h2 → h32 for rigid_w5), suggesting potential overfitting or instability — yet no regularization or early-stopping mechanism is reported.\n\nReproducibility is compromised: no random seeds, optimizer settings, or data splits are provided. Benchmarking is absent — no comparison to analytical solutions, finite-element references, or prior literature on rigid-body or linear elasticity problems (if applicable). The raw error tables lack units, normalization, or physical interpretation, rendering them unverifiable against first-principles constraints (e.g., energy bounds, symmetry preservation).\n\nNext steps:\n1. Define explicit pass/fail criteria for held-out error (e.g., ≤1e-4) and report whether each configuration meets them.\n2. Add convergence plots: train/val error vs. epoch, and sensitivity to width/depth.\n3. Include at least one benchmark against a known solution (e.g., analytical stress field, conservation law residual).\n4. Specify architecture details (activation functions, weight initialization, loss formulation) and random seeds for reproducibility.\n5. Interpret errors in physical context — e.g., do they violate equilibrium conditions or material stability?"
